[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. The non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function are [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe zeros in the critical strip $0 < \text{Re}(s) < 1$. It's a classical result [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthat there are infinitely many non-trivial zeros. The question is whether $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{Im}(z_n) \to \infty$ as $n \to \infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is actually a well-known result. Let me think through the proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. The Riemann zeta function $\zeta(s)$ is meromorphic with only a simple pole [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mat $s=1$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. The non-trivial zeros lie in the critical strip $0 < \sigma < 1$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. There are infinitely many non-trivial zeros.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. For any $T > 0$, the number of zeros in the critical strip with $|\text{Im}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(s)| \leq T$ is finite.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mPoint 4 is the crucial one. If for every $T$, the number of non-trivial zeros [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwith $|\text{Im}(z)| \leq T$ is finite, then the non-trivial zeros can be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124menumerated (ordered by imaginary part), and $\text{Im}(z_n) \to \infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWhy is point 4 true? This follows from the fact that $\zeta(s)$ is analytic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(except at $s=1$) and the zeros of a non-constant analytic function are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124misolated. In any compact subset of the domain of analyticity, there can only be [0m[38;2;124;124;124mfinitely many zeros (otherwise they'd accumulate, and by the identity theorem [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe function would be identically zero).[0m

